    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_1b2ee3ac3f9943fd101bbf2dfd7f177dfe4d60e8a5102ac3765843108fb40a7c",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The construction separates local gain from global capacity control. A finite band of primes defines square-free multiplicative weights whose local contributions factor into an Euler product; logarithmic expansion and prime-density integration establish its asymptotic gain. The full square-free support generated by that finite band is finite but potentially enormous, so blockwise prime-occupancy caps select a divisor-closed subset of size at most N. An exponential tilt proves that the discarded weighted tail is negligible, while a later complementary-divisor estimate shows that the retained support also loses negligible mass in the divisor range excluded by the resonator argument.",
