    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_7dc954ca980a793a0859503abf8b07cd95bab4de60bd86abf1211614b6e5dbdd",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The weighted GCD theorem supplies a large optimized quadratic form for every sufficiently large dimension. Defining Gamma(N) for the uniform GCD average and Lambda(N) for the optimized Rayleigh quotient gives Gamma(N)<=Lambda(N), while a cited reverse comparison controls Lambda(N) by a logarithmic factor times the running maximum of Gamma(n). Consequently the weighted gain cannot remain hidden at all uniform scales: it forces arbitrarily large unweighted configurations and shows that the exponent constant in the earlier general GCD upper bound cannot be taken below one.",
