    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_f8ef0317221e6ed922cf29f58d27a7361c10ab765b9cfb8004da918513f376c8",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Prime-supported square-free weights make the weighted GCD kernel large by rewarding shared divisors. The divisor-closed support carrying that gain is then compressed into short logarithmic clusters, with nearby squared weights aggregated into resonator coefficients. Because the smoothed moment sees logarithmic ratios rather than absolute integer sizes, clustered representatives retain the multiplicative relations to the resolution needed by the Fourier kernel. Expanding the zeta-resonator numerator gives a positive triple sum, so selected near-relations among representatives can be compared back to exact divisor relations in the original support and recover the GCD gain inside the moment ratio.",
